 2.1 **Rooted tree collapses and changes of tree.** The zero/one-handle graph has one old root vertex N and one vertex for every new ball; edges are the one-handles. It is connected because higher-index attaching regions are connected and cannot merge components. Choose a spanning tree T. Starting at its leaves, the union of a leaf ball and its incident tree tube is a ball with one retained attaching disk. Absorb it into its neighbor's collar using the explicit ball-plus-tube product chart. Every other foot and every higher attaching embedding is carried by this very chart. Repetition removes all new zero-handles. The support stays off the protected vertical collar. Reversing gives the outgoing tree collapse. Two trees are compared by single-edge exchanges. If T′ differs from T, choose f∈T′−T. The unique cycle in T∪{f} contains an edge e∈T−T′; replacing e by f reduces the number of differing edges. Finite induction reaches T′. For this one exchange restrict to the cycle plus its attached tree branches; first absorb the branches by the same leaf-ball products. The cycle is now a cyclic sequence of balls and tubes attached to the root component by a tree path. Collapse all but the two edges e,f. The remaining model is two balls joined by two tubes, with its incoming root collar retained. Collapsing the first tube or the second tube gives the same ball-plus-one-handle model with different retained core paths. To see the actual comparison, cut both tubes at transverse three-disks. The cut piece is a ball with the four named disk faces e_±,f_±. Moving the chosen joining tube across the other disk face is the k=1 disk push of the actual handle-slide proof. Other incident feet are moved along the displayed tube by parallel disk patches. Each such movement is a one-handle slide over the surviving cycle handle; its thickening is the product D³ of the corresponding patch. A higher attaching strip is carried by the same product chart. Restoring the absorbed branches transports